'udaipur-3-day-itinerary' => [
 'title'=>'Udaipur Itinerary for 3 Days: A Practical First-Time Travel Plan',
 'description'=>'Plan a memorable 3-day Udaipur trip with a day-wise itinerary covering City Palace, Lake Pichola, Sajjangarh, local food, shopping and travel tips.',
 'keyword'=>'Udaipur itinerary for 3 days','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'9 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/udaipur-3-day-itinerary.webp',
 'intro'=>'Udaipur rewards travellers who slow down. The City of Lakes combines grand palaces, old neighbourhoods, lively bazaars and Aravalli viewpoints in a compact area. Three days are enough for the essential sights without turning the holiday into a checklist. This plan groups nearby attractions, leaves room for sunset experiences and includes practical decisions about transport, tickets and meals.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['Day 1: Old City, City Palace and Lake Pichola','Begin near Jagdish Temple before the lanes become crowded. Walk to the City Palace complex and allow at least two to three hours for courtyards, balconies, museum galleries and views across Lake Pichola. A local guide can add context, but independent travellers should follow the marked museum route. After lunch, explore Gangaur Ghat and the shops around Lal Ghat. Reserve an evening boat ride from the authorised counter; sunset departures are popular, so reach early. Finish with dinner at a rooftop restaurant overlooking the illuminated palace area.'],
 ['Day 2: Saheliyon Ki Bari, museums and Sajjangarh sunset','Start with Saheliyon Ki Bari, a peaceful garden known for fountains and marble pavilions. Continue towards Fateh Sagar Lake and choose between the Vintage and Classic Car Museum, Bharatiya Lok Kala Mandal or the Maharana Pratap memorial according to your interests. In late afternoon, drive to Sajjangarh Monsoon Palace. The road climbs above the city and gives a broad view of the lakes and surrounding hills. Carry water and a light layer in winter. Return before late evening because transport availability falls after sunset.'],
 ['Day 3: Eklingji, Nagda or a relaxed local day','For heritage and architecture, take a half-day trip to Eklingji and the Sas-Bahu temples at Nagda. Travellers who prefer a slower schedule can visit Badi Lake, Shilpgram and lesser-known cafés around Fateh Sagar. Keep the final afternoon flexible for shopping. Hathipole is useful for textiles, paintings and souvenirs, while Bada Bazaar has traditional clothing and everyday local commerce. Compare quality and ask for a bill on higher-value purchases.'],
 ['Where to stay and how to move around','Choose the Old City for atmosphere and walkability, Fateh Sagar for quieter lake access, or areas near the railway station for easier transfers and mid-range value. Auto-rickshaws work well for short rides; a reserved cab is more convenient for Sajjangarh and outstation temples. Old City lanes are narrow, so walking is often faster. Agree on fares before the ride when a meter is not used.'],
 ['Best time, budget and booking tips','October to March offers comfortable sightseeing weather. July to September brings green hills and fuller lakes, though showers can interrupt plans. April to June is hot; begin early and rest at midday. Your budget depends mainly on hotel style, private transport and boat tickets. Book accommodation and popular experiences early for long weekends, festivals and the winter wedding season. Always verify current entry times and official ticket prices before departure.']
 ]],
'best-places-to-visit-in-udaipur' => [
 'title'=>'15 Best Places to Visit in Udaipur for a Complete City Experience','description'=>'Discover the best places to visit in Udaipur, from City Palace and Lake Pichola to Badi Lake, Sajjangarh, temples, museums and local markets.','keyword'=>'best places to visit in Udaipur','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'10 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/best-places-udaipur.webp','intro'=>'The best places to visit in Udaipur are spread across historic lakefronts, palace complexes, gardens and hills. A useful sightseeing list should do more than name attractions: it should show which places combine well, when to visit and what kind of traveller will enjoy them. The following selection balances famous landmarks with quieter local experiences.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['1–5: The essential landmarks','City Palace is the natural starting point for its museum, architecture and Lake Pichola views. Jagdish Temple sits a short walk away and remains an active place of worship. Gangaur Ghat gives direct access to the historic waterfront. A regulated Lake Pichola boat ride reveals the palace skyline from the water. Bagore Ki Haveli completes the core Old City circuit, especially when its evening cultural programme is operating. Together these attractions can fill one well-paced day.'],
 ['6–10: Lakes, gardens and viewpoints','Fateh Sagar Lake is ideal for an evening promenade and casual food stops. Saheliyon Ki Bari offers landscaped gardens and ornamental fountains. Sajjangarh Monsoon Palace provides the city’s broadest sunset panorama, while the nearby biological park suits families with more time. Badi Lake is quieter and feels removed from the centre; responsible visitors should carry back all waste and avoid risky cliff edges. These locations are easiest with a cab or planned auto route.'],
 ['11–15: Culture, craft and nearby heritage','Shilpgram introduces regional crafts and performance traditions. Bharatiya Lok Kala Mandal focuses on folk culture and puppetry. The Ahar Cenotaphs appeal to architecture and history enthusiasts. Eklingji Temple and the Nagda temple ruins form a rewarding half-day excursion north of Udaipur. Vintage vehicle fans can include the Vintage and Classic Car Museum. Check weekly closures and current visitor rules when designing the day.'],
 ['How to arrange your sightseeing','Group City Palace, Jagdish Temple, Gangaur Ghat and Bagore Ki Haveli because they are walkable. Put Fateh Sagar, Saheliyon Ki Bari and nearby museums together. Reserve Sajjangarh for a clear late afternoon, and keep Badi Lake or the temple excursion for a separate half-day. This reduces repeated travel and leaves more time at each attraction.'],
 ['Responsible travel advice','Dress respectfully at religious sites, ask permission before photographing people and use official counters for tickets. Avoid feeding wildlife and do not leave plastic around lakes or viewpoints. Udaipur’s heritage core is also a living neighbourhood, so keep noise low in small lanes and respect private homes. A thoughtful itinerary supports local guides, drivers, artisans and family-run businesses.']
 ]],
'udaipur-best-time-to-visit' => [
 'title'=>'Best Time to Visit Udaipur: Weather, Seasons, Festivals and Costs','description'=>'Compare Udaipur weather by season and choose the best time for sightseeing, honeymoon trips, photography, festivals and affordable travel.','keyword'=>'best time to visit Udaipur','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'8 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/best-time-udaipur.webp','intro'=>'The best time to visit Udaipur depends on whether you value mild weather, green landscapes, fewer visitors or lower hotel prices. Winter is the easiest season for sightseeing, monsoon transforms the Aravalli hills, and summer can suit travellers who plan short early-morning outings. Understanding these trade-offs makes it easier to choose dates and budget realistically.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['October to March: popular sightseeing season','Days are generally pleasant and evenings can feel cool, particularly from December to February. This is the preferred period for walking through the Old City, visiting forts and watching sunsets. Demand rises around holidays, festivals and weddings, so hotels and private vehicles may cost more. Reserve early and carry a light jacket for late boat rides or hill viewpoints.'],
 ['July to September: lakes and green hills','Monsoon rain revives the surrounding landscape and often creates dramatic clouds for photography. Showers may be brief or may disrupt an outdoor plan, so keep one museum or café option in reserve. Roads can be slower during heavy rain. The season works especially well for repeat visitors, couples and travellers more interested in atmosphere than covering every attraction.'],
 ['April to June: quieter but hot','Afternoon heat can be intense. Start sightseeing near opening time, drink safe water and use the middle of the day for rest, indoor museums or a long lunch. Some hotels offer better value than in winter, but air-conditioned accommodation becomes important. This period is not ideal for travellers sensitive to heat, older visitors or very young children.'],
 ['Festivals and event planning','Mewar Festival and other cultural occasions can add processions and performances to a trip, but exact dates and arrangements change annually. Diwali, Holi, New Year and long weekends also affect availability. Verify dates through official tourism or event sources before booking non-refundable travel. Wedding-season traffic may increase transfer times around popular hotels.'],
 ['Choosing dates for your travel style','First-time families usually find November to February easiest. Honeymoon couples may enjoy winter or the romantic monsoon landscape. Photographers can choose monsoon clouds or clear winter mornings. Budget travellers can look at shoulder periods such as late September or March. Whatever the month, confirm the short-range weather forecast and current attraction timings before leaving.']
 ]],
'udaipur-honeymoon-guide' => [
 'title'=>'Udaipur Honeymoon Guide: Romantic Places, Stays and a 4-Day Plan','description'=>'Plan an Udaipur honeymoon with romantic lake experiences, a 4-day couple itinerary, stay areas, dining ideas and practical booking advice.','keyword'=>'Udaipur honeymoon package','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'9 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/udaipur-honeymoon.webp','intro'=>'An Udaipur honeymoon can combine palace heritage with quiet lake mornings, rooftop dinners and short drives into the Aravalli hills. The best couple trip is not necessarily the busiest one. A comfortable hotel, realistic transfer times and one memorable experience each day usually create a better holiday than rushing between every attraction.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['A balanced four-day honeymoon plan','Use the arrival day for check-in, Gangaur Ghat and a relaxed dinner. On day two, explore City Palace, Jagdish Temple and Lake Pichola with a pre-planned boat ride. Day three can combine Saheliyon Ki Bari, Fateh Sagar and Sajjangarh sunset. Keep day four for Badi Lake, a spa session, shopping or a private excursion to Eklingji and Nagda before departure.'],
 ['Romantic experiences worth planning','A legal boat ride, an early walk along a quieter lakefront, a heritage-hotel meal and a sunset viewpoint each offer different moods. Ask hotels about private dining only after checking the full cost, weather backup and cancellation terms. Avoid unsafe isolated viewpoints after dark. Genuine romance comes from unhurried time, not only premium add-ons.'],
 ['Where couples should stay','Old City heritage properties place you close to palace views but may involve stairs, narrow access lanes and limited parking. Fateh Sagar and surrounding areas feel calmer and are easier for cars. Luxury lake-facing resorts offer privacy but can be distant from everyday restaurants and markets. Match the area to your mobility, desired view and daily transport budget.'],
 ['Budget and booking decisions','Separate must-have spending from optional upgrades. Prioritise a comfortable room, reliable transfers and the experience you care about most. Ask whether breakfast, taxes, transfers and special arrangements are included. During peak wedding and holiday dates, refundable reservations are valuable. Never transfer money to an unverified personal account solely on the basis of social media images.'],
 ['Small details that improve the trip','Share dietary preferences in advance, carry identity documents, keep digital and offline copies of bookings and leave extra time for traffic. Comfortable shoes are useful in palace complexes. A light layer helps on winter evenings, while monsoon travellers should carry compact rain protection. Tell your planner if the trip celebrates a specific occasion so the schedule can protect that time.']
 ]],
'rajasthan-road-trip-itinerary' => [
 'title'=>'Rajasthan Road Trip Itinerary: 10 Days Through Jaipur, Jodhpur and Udaipur','description'=>'Follow a practical 10-day Rajasthan road trip covering Jaipur, Pushkar, Jodhpur and Udaipur with routes, stays and driving tips.','keyword'=>'Rajasthan road trip itinerary','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'11 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/rajasthan-road-trip.webp','intro'=>'A Rajasthan road trip works best when distances, hotel check-ins and sightseeing hours are treated realistically. Jaipur, Pushkar, Jodhpur and Udaipur create a rewarding route with forts, lakes, markets and changing landscapes. Ten days allow useful two-night stays and reduce the fatigue caused by changing hotels every morning.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['Days 1–3: Jaipur','Arrive and use the first evening for local markets or a relaxed meal. Dedicate the next day to Amber Fort, Panna Meena ka Kund and the northern viewpoints. On day three, explore City Palace, Jantar Mantar, Hawa Mahal frontage and selected bazaars. Start early, use official parking and avoid packing too many distant stops into the same day.'],
 ['Day 4: Jaipur to Pushkar','Drive to Pushkar after breakfast and check in before walking around the lake and market. Respect local restrictions around ghats and temples. Pushkar is compact, so one night is sufficient for most road trips. Travellers wanting Ajmer can add the city only if traffic and religious-site schedules leave enough time.'],
 ['Days 5–6: Jodhpur','Continue to Jodhpur and allow the arrival evening for the Clock Tower market. The next morning belongs to Mehrangarh Fort and Jaswant Thada. Later, explore the blue lanes with a local guide or visit Mandore. Parking inside dense neighbourhoods is limited; use a designated point and walk.'],
 ['Days 7–10: Udaipur','Drive south towards Udaipur, optionally stopping at Ranakpur when temple timing and route conditions work. Spend two full days on City Palace, Lake Pichola, Fateh Sagar, Saheliyon Ki Bari and Sajjangarh. Keep the final morning relaxed before departure. An extra night can be assigned to Udaipur or Jodhpur according to flight connections.'],
 ['Road safety and practical planning','Use a well-maintained vehicle, licensed driver and seat belts for every passenger. Avoid unnecessary night driving, especially on unfamiliar rural roads. Build rest stops into long sectors, store offline maps and keep emergency contacts accessible. Confirm route conditions during monsoon and allow buffer time around festivals. Distances shown by navigation apps do not account for meals, roadwork or scenic stops.']
 ]],
'jaipur-2-day-itinerary' => [
 'title'=>'Jaipur Itinerary for 2 Days: Forts, Palaces, Markets and Food','description'=>'See Jaipur in two days with an efficient itinerary for Amber Fort, City Palace, Hawa Mahal, Jantar Mantar, bazaars and local food.','keyword'=>'Jaipur itinerary for 2 days','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'8 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/jaipur-2-day-itinerary.webp','intro'=>'A two-day Jaipur itinerary must separate the hill forts from the dense historic centre. Doing so cuts travel time and prevents repeated journeys through busy corridors. Begin early, choose only the attractions that match your interests and keep a realistic break for meals and afternoon heat.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['Day 1: Amber and north Jaipur','Reach Amber Fort close to opening time and explore its courtyards and viewpoints. Continue to Panna Meena ka Kund where access rules should be respected. Stop at Jal Mahal only from the permitted roadside viewing area. Depending on time, add Jaigarh Fort or Nahargarh, not necessarily both. Reserve sunset only after checking closing and vehicle timings.'],
 ['Day 2: Pink City heritage','Start near Hawa Mahal and walk towards Jantar Mantar and City Palace. These monuments are close, but ticket queues and museum galleries take time. After lunch, browse Johari Bazaar or Bapu Bazaar for textiles, jewellery and souvenirs. Use a registered guide if you want deeper historical interpretation, and agree on any shopping stops in advance.'],
 ['Food and evening ideas','Try established local restaurants for kachori, dal baati churma or sweets, taking hygiene and dietary comfort into account. Jaipur also has contemporary cafés and rooftop dining. Avoid planning a distant dinner immediately after a sunset fort visit because traffic may delay the return. Ask your hotel for current, nearby recommendations.'],
 ['Where to stay','The Pink City offers heritage atmosphere, while C-Scheme and surrounding central districts provide restaurants and easier modern access. Areas near the airport work for early flights but increase sightseeing travel. Select the hotel after checking actual map location, parking, lift access and recent verified reviews rather than relying only on neighbourhood labels.'],
 ['Useful travel tips','Wear comfortable footwear, carry drinking water and dress modestly for temples. Jaipur traffic can make short map distances slow. Keep buffer time, use official ticket channels and confirm photography rules. For a third day, add Albert Hall Museum, Galta Ji, a craft workshop or a slower market experience.']
 ]],
'jaisalmer-desert-camp-guide' => [
 'title'=>'Jaisalmer Desert Camp Guide: Sam vs Khuri, Costs and Safety Tips','description'=>'Choose the right Jaisalmer desert camp with a clear comparison of Sam and Khuri dunes, activities, inclusions, safety and booking checks.','keyword'=>'Jaisalmer desert camp','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'9 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/jaisalmer-desert-camp.webp','intro'=>'A Jaisalmer desert camp can be the highlight of a Rajasthan journey, but the experience varies widely. Location, tent quality, crowd levels, meals, entertainment and transport all influence value. Compare what is included and choose a camp that follows safe, responsible practices rather than selecting only from staged photographs.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['Sam dunes or Khuri dunes?','Sam has the largest selection of camps, activities and group entertainment. It is convenient for first-time visitors but can feel busy in peak season. Khuri generally offers a quieter village atmosphere with fewer large properties. Neither area is automatically better; the right choice depends on whether you prefer organised activity or a more restrained desert stay.'],
 ['What a camp package may include','Common packages combine Jaisalmer transfers, welcome refreshments, camel or jeep activity, sunset viewing, cultural performance, dinner, tent stay and breakfast. Inclusions are not standard. Ask for the exact tent category, private-bathroom arrangement, pickup point, activity duration, taxes and cancellation rules in writing. Clarify whether a jeep ride is shared and whether any supplements apply.'],
 ['Safety and comfort checks','Verify the operator’s identity, property location and recent guest feedback. Families should ask about barriers, lighting and night supervision. Desert temperatures can change sharply after sunset, so pack layers in winter. People with back, neck or mobility concerns should discuss camel and off-road rides with a qualified professional and choose a comfortable alternative when needed.'],
 ['Responsible desert travel','Stay on authorised routes, avoid littering and do not pressure animals or handlers for unsafe performances. Loud music, off-route driving and waste can damage fragile desert environments. Choose operators that manage sewage and rubbish responsibly, employ local teams fairly and present culture respectfully rather than as a forced spectacle.'],
 ['Combining camp and city sightseeing','Spend one day at Jaisalmer Fort, Patwon Ki Haveli, Gadisar Lake and the old lanes, then move towards the desert by mid-afternoon. One camp night is enough for many travellers. Keep the following morning flexible because breakfast and transfers can run later than expected. Confirm current road and weather conditions before departure.']
 ]],
'jodhpur-travel-guide' => [
 'title'=>'Jodhpur Travel Guide: Top Places, Food, Blue City Walks and Itinerary','description'=>'Use this Jodhpur travel guide to plan Mehrangarh Fort, Blue City walks, markets, local food, stays and a practical 2-day itinerary.','keyword'=>'Jodhpur travel guide','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'9 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/jodhpur-travel-guide.webp','intro'=>'Jodhpur combines one of India’s most imposing forts with blue-painted neighbourhoods, busy bazaars and distinctive Marwari food. Two well-planned days cover the main experiences. The key is to visit Mehrangarh early and explore residential lanes with respect, preferably with a knowledgeable local guide.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['Day 1: Mehrangarh and the old city','Start at Mehrangarh Fort and allow several hours for exhibits, ramparts and views. Walk or drive down to Jaswant Thada, then rest before entering the Clock Tower and Sardar Market area. The market is best experienced slowly. Keep valuables secure in crowds and ask before photographing shopkeepers or residents.'],
 ['Day 2: Blue City and wider Jodhpur','Book a responsible guided walk through selected blue lanes. These are living communities rather than a photo set. Later, choose Umaid Bhawan Palace Museum, Mandore Gardens or Rao Jodha Desert Rock Park based on your interests and the season. The rock park is most comfortable outside the hottest hours.'],
 ['Food to try','Popular choices include mirchi bada, mawa kachori and traditional thali dishes. Pick busy, reputable outlets with good hygiene. Ask about spice levels and ingredients if you have dietary needs. Carry safe water and avoid trying too many heavy foods on a long driving day.'],
 ['Stay areas and transport','The old city places you near markets and fort views but includes narrow lanes and limited vehicle access. Ratanada and other modern areas can be easier for parking, trains and airport transfers. Autos suit short trips, while a reserved car helps with Mandore and outlying stops. Agree on pickup points clearly.'],
 ['When to visit and what to pack','October to March is comfortable for outdoor sightseeing. Summer requires early starts, sun protection and midday breaks. Monsoon can soften the landscape but may affect walking surfaces. Pack supportive shoes, modest clothing for religious sites and a light layer in winter. Confirm attraction hours and current local conditions.']
 ]],
'mount-abu-weekend-trip' => [
 'title'=>'Mount Abu Weekend Trip: 2-Day Itinerary from Udaipur','description'=>'Plan a Mount Abu weekend trip from Udaipur with Nakki Lake, Dilwara Temples, viewpoints, travel time and responsible tourism tips.','keyword'=>'Mount Abu weekend trip','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'8 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/mount-abu-weekend.webp','intro'=>'Mount Abu offers a cooler hill setting within Rajasthan and works well as a two-day break from Udaipur. The road journey, temple timings and weekend crowds need realistic planning. A balanced visit combines Dilwara architecture, Nakki Lake and one or two viewpoints without spending the entire trip in traffic.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['Getting there from Udaipur','Road travel is the most direct option for many visitors. Start early and keep time for a meal or rest stop. Abu Road is the nearest major railway station, followed by a hill transfer. Travel duration varies with traffic, roadwork and weather, so avoid planning a fixed activity immediately after the estimated arrival.'],
 ['Day 1: Nakki Lake and nearby sights','Check in, have lunch and explore Nakki Lake and the central market. Use only authorised boating services and follow life-jacket instructions. Toad Rock may appeal to active travellers, but footwear and weather matter. Select a permitted sunset point and leave enough time to return before roads become congested.'],
 ['Day 2: Dilwara Temples and viewpoints','Visit the Dilwara Temples according to current visitor hours and rules. Photography and belongings may be restricted, so follow on-site instructions. Later, choose Guru Shikhar, Achalgarh or a nature stop depending on time. Trying to cover every point often reduces the quality of the visit.'],
 ['Where to stay and what to expect','Central stays make the lake and market walkable, while properties farther out may offer quieter surroundings. Check access roads, parking, heating or cooling and recent reviews. Weekends and holidays bring heavier demand. Book refundable rooms early and ask whether quoted rates include taxes and meals.'],
 ['Responsible hill travel','Do not throw waste from vehicles or leave plastic near trails and lakes. Avoid feeding monkeys and keep food secured. Drive cautiously on bends, especially in fog or rain. Respect forest barriers and closing times. A low-impact visit helps protect the fragile hill environment and improves the experience for residents and travellers.']
 ]],
'pushkar-travel-guide' => [
 'title'=>'Pushkar Travel Guide: Ghats, Temples, Cafés and a One-Day Plan','description'=>'Plan a respectful Pushkar visit with a one-day itinerary for the lake, Brahma Temple, markets, cafés, sunset and practical local tips.','keyword'=>'Pushkar travel guide','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'8 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/pushkar-travel-guide.webp','intro'=>'Pushkar is compact, spiritual and easy to combine with Jaipur or Ajmer. Its ghats, temples, market lanes and café culture can fill one relaxed day. Visitors should remember that Pushkar Lake is a sacred space. Respectful behaviour, suitable clothing and awareness of common tourist pressure make the experience more meaningful.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['A practical one-day itinerary','Arrive in the morning and begin with a quiet walk near the lake. Follow footwear, photography and access rules at the ghats. Visit the Brahma Temple during permitted hours, then browse the main market. After lunch, rest at a café or return to your hotel before choosing a safe sunset viewpoint.'],
 ['Temple and ghat etiquette','Dress modestly, speak softly and never photograph rituals or individuals without consent. Leave shoes only at designated points and keep valuables secure. If someone offers a ceremony or blessing, ask clearly whether any payment is expected and decide without pressure. Official local guidance can help resolve uncertainty.'],
 ['Shopping and food','Pushkar’s market sells textiles, leather-style goods, jewellery and souvenirs. Compare quality and prices, and retain bills for valuable purchases. Many eateries are vegetarian in keeping with the town’s traditions. Choose clean, established businesses and communicate allergies or ingredient restrictions clearly.'],
 ['Pushkar Fair planning','The annual fair period can bring cultural activity, livestock trading, performances and very high visitor demand. Dates and programme details vary, so confirm them through authoritative sources. Book accommodation and transfers well in advance, expect traffic restrictions and protect yourself from unverified ticket or tent offers.'],
 ['How Pushkar fits a Rajasthan route','From Jaipur, Pushkar commonly works as an overnight stop before Jodhpur. From Udaipur or Jodhpur, it can be included only when driving time suits the wider itinerary. One night gives a calmer experience than a rushed detour. Travellers with more time can explore surrounding nature with a responsible local operator.']
 ]],
'rajasthan-family-trip' => [
 'title'=>'Rajasthan Family Trip: Kid-Friendly 7-Day Itinerary and Planning Tips','description'=>'Plan a comfortable Rajasthan family trip with a seven-day Jaipur and Udaipur itinerary, child-friendly pacing, stays, food and safety tips.','keyword'=>'Rajasthan family trip','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'9 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/rajasthan-family-trip.webp','intro'=>'A Rajasthan family trip should prioritise comfortable pacing, short transfers and attractions that interest different age groups. Seven days work well for Jaipur and Udaipur without constant hotel changes. Families can add interactive museums, gardens and boat experiences while limiting long midday walks and steep climbs.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['Days 1–3: Jaipur with children','Use the arrival day for rest. Visit Amber Fort early the next morning, then make a short stop at Jal Mahal’s permitted viewing area. On day three, choose City Palace and Jantar Mantar, followed by an easy market visit. Avoid adding multiple hill forts unless children are enthusiastic and comfortable with stairs.'],
 ['Day 4: Transfer to Udaipur','A flight or train may reduce road fatigue depending on schedules and total cost. If driving, plan safe rest stops and do not rely on optimistic map timing. Keep the arrival evening free for the lakefront or hotel. A relaxed transfer day prevents tiredness from affecting the rest of the holiday.'],
 ['Days 5–7: Udaipur at a family pace','Explore City Palace in the morning, with breaks for galleries and courtyards. Plan a regulated boat ride only when weather and comfort permit. Use the next day for Saheliyon Ki Bari, Fateh Sagar and one museum. Keep the final day flexible for departure, shopping or a short cultural activity.'],
 ['Hotels, rooms and meals','Confirm lift access, extra-bed dimensions, interconnected-room availability and breakfast timings directly with the hotel. A swimming pool is useful only when seasonal and supervised. Carry familiar snacks for transfers, but also let children try mild local food from reputable restaurants. Communicate allergies clearly.'],
 ['Safety and packing','Use age-appropriate car seats where available, insist on seat belts and supervise children near fort walls, roads and water. Pack sun hats, hydration supplies, comfortable footwear and basic prescribed medicines. Discuss health concerns with a qualified professional. Keep identification, emergency numbers and hotel details accessible offline.']
 ]],
'rajasthan-travel-budget' => [
 'title'=>'Rajasthan Trip Cost: A Realistic Budget Guide for 5, 7 and 10 Days','description'=>'Estimate Rajasthan trip cost across budget, mid-range and premium travel, including hotels, transport, food, sightseeing and practical savings.','keyword'=>'Rajasthan trip cost','date'=>'2026-09-12','read'=>'10 min read','image'=>'../assets/images/blog/rajasthan-trip-cost.webp','intro'=>'Rajasthan trip cost depends less on the number of attractions than on travel style, hotel category, city combination and private transport. A useful budget separates fixed items from daily spending and keeps a buffer for taxes, guides, activities and seasonal price changes. The figures in any online guide should be treated as planning ranges, not live quotations.',
 'sections'=>[
 ['The main cost categories','Accommodation and intercity transport usually take the largest share. Add local sightseeing vehicles, meals, monument tickets, guides, activities, shopping, tips and travel insurance according to your needs. Airfare can change quickly and should be budgeted separately. Always compare the complete payable amount, including taxes and transfers.'],
 ['Budget, mid-range and premium styles','Budget travellers may use trains, simple guesthouses and shared local transport. Mid-range trips often combine comfortable hotels with a private car for selected days. Premium travel may include heritage properties, larger vehicles, curated guides and private experiences. The best choice is the one that protects safety and comfort while matching priorities.'],
 ['Planning for 5, 7 or 10 days','A five-day trip should focus on two nearby bases. Seven days can cover Jaipur and Udaipur comfortably or Jaipur, Jodhpur and one additional stop at a faster pace. Ten days allow a broader circuit with sensible two-night stays. More destinations increase transfer costs and reduce usable sightseeing time.'],
 ['How to control costs','Travel outside major holiday periods, book refundable rooms early and compare train, flight and car combinations. Group nearby attractions and avoid keeping a private vehicle idle in fully walkable zones. Ask for written package inclusions. Do not sacrifice vehicle condition, licensed operation or safe accommodation for a small saving.'],
 ['Build a sensible buffer','Keep a contingency for weather changes, medical needs, route adjustments and last-minute transport. Prices and entry fees change, so verify them close to travel. A planner can produce a current quotation once dates, passenger count, hotel preference, pickup city and required experiences are known.']
 ]]
];

